.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_METHOD 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_method \- return the HTTP method of a request
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttp_method_t chttpsvr_req_method(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the HTTP method of req as a chttp_method_t enum value.  The
return value is always one of the seven real methods: CHTTP_GET,
CHTTP_POST, CHTTP_PUT, CHTTP_DELETE, CHTTP_PATCH, CHTTP_HEAD and
CHTTP_OPTIONS.  The function never returns CHTTP_ANY.  This is true
even when the route that matched was registered with CHTTP_ANY.

A request whose method the server does not recognize at all never
reaches any handler.  This includes a handler that CHTTP_ANY
registered.  Such a method is a WebDAV verb such as PROPFIND, a verb
such as TRACE or CONNECT, or a custom verb.  The server rejects the
request with 501 Not Implemented before the routing runs.  See the
notes on CHTTP_ANY in chttpsvr_register_handler(3).  This is what makes
"always one of the seven real methods" a complete guarantee, and not
only the common case.

The function returns CHTTP_GET when req is NULL.  Most other request
accessors return NULL for a req of NULL.  chttp_method_t is an integer
enum with no sentinel value, so it cannot do that.  Guard against NULL
before you call this function when req can be NULL.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_path (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_header (3)
